Day 05 – Docker Hub & Container Troubleshooting

Overview

Learned how Docker Hub works as a container image registry and practiced publishing, downloading, and deploying Docker images. Also performed a simulated company troubleshooting task to identify and fix an Nginx deployment issue.

1. Docker Hub – Theory

- Docker Hub is a cloud-based registry for storing and sharing Docker images.
- "docker pull" downloads images from a registry.
- "docker push" uploads images to a registry.
- Public repositories allow other users to access and download published images.
- Image tags help identify different image versions.

2. Hands-on Implementation

Pulled the Nginx image:

sudo docker pull nginx

Verified local images:

sudo docker images

Created a public Docker Hub repository: "aman1030/my-nginx"

Tagged the image:

sudo docker tag nginx:latest aman1030/my-nginx:v1

Logged in to Docker Hub:

sudo docker login -u aman1030

Resolved an authentication issue by using a Personal Access Token with repository Read & Write permissions.

Pushed the image:

sudo docker push aman1030/my-nginx:v1

Pulled the published image:

sudo docker pull aman1030/my-nginx:v1

Deployed a container using the published image:

sudo docker run -d --name hub-nginx-test -p 9093:80 aman1030/my-nginx:v1

Verified the Nginx Welcome Page at "http://localhost:9093".

3. Practical Troubleshooting

Scenario: Nginx was running, but the application was inaccessible.

Investigation:

sudo docker ps -a

Identified incorrect port mapping:

- Incorrect: "9094:8080"
- Correct: "9094:80"

Root Cause: Nginx listens on container port 80 by default, but the container was configured to use port 8080.

Resolution:

Removed the incorrectly configured container:

sudo docker rm -f ticket-nginx

Recreated it with the correct port mapping:

sudo docker run -d --name ticket-nginx -p 9094:80 nginx

Verification: Successfully accessed the Nginx Welcome Page at "http://localhost:9094".

Key Learnings

- Docker Hub image publishing and distribution.
- Image tagging, authentication, pulling, and pushing.
- Personal Access Token permissions.
- Running containers from published images.
- Investigating deployment issues and identifying root causes.
- Fixing port mapping and verifying application availability.

